{% set local_problem %}
<p>Decatur sits in one of the most challenging drainage environments in North Alabama. Tennessee River wraps around the city. Wheeler Lake sits east. Wheeler Wildlife Refuge covers thousands of acres of wetland nearby. Water is everywhere — including under your house.</p>

<p>That means Decatur's water table is naturally elevated. In low-lying areas — <a href="/recent-projects/">near Point Mallard, parts of Southwest Decatur, along the river</a> — groundwater can rise to 3-4 feet below foundation level during wet periods. When TVA releases water through the dam system after regional rainfall, levels rise even higher.</p>

<p>Here's the problem: when your foundation is already dealing with groundwater pressure, adding roof runoff to the mix makes everything worse. Standard downspout extensions that dump water 6 feet from your home aren't adequate. That water lands on clay soil that won't absorb it, pools against your foundation, and adds hydrostatic pressure to walls already under stress.</p>

<p>Underground drains change the equation entirely. Instead of depositing water near your foundation and hoping for the best, they route it 20, 30, or 50+ feet away through sealed pipes. The water never touches foundation soil. It exits at a discharge point far from your home — at property edge, into a storm drain, wherever it can safely disperse.</p>

<p>For <a href="/recent-projects/">Decatur homeowners</a> dealing with wet basements, musty crawl spaces, foundation cracks, or chronic moisture, underground drains are often the only solution that actually works long-term.</p>

<p><strong>The investment perspective:</strong> Underground drains for a typical Decatur home run $1,200-$2,400. Foundation repair runs $10,000-30,000. We've seen homeowners spend years dealing with moisture problems, trying cheaper fixes, before finally installing underground drains and solving the issue permanently. Should've done it first.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes connecting to your downspouts and carrying water through a sealed, sloped channel to a discharge point far from your home. They work continuously and automatically — every time it rains, water routes away before it can threaten your foundation.</p>

<p><strong>What we install:</strong> 4-inch Schedule 40 PVC pipe with smooth interior walls. Proper slope (1-2% grade) ensuring water flows continuously rather than pooling. Pop-up emitters or daylight exits at discharge points. Clean-out access for future maintenance.</p>

<p><strong>What we don't install:</strong> Corrugated flex pipe. This is critical. That black corrugated drainage pipe from Home Depot seems like a cheap alternative, but the corrugations trap sediment and debris. Roots grow into the ridges. Within 3-5 years, most corrugated pipe is partially or completely clogged. We dig up failed corrugated systems in Decatur constantly. Smooth PVC costs more upfront but actually works long-term.</p>

<p><strong>The distinction to understand:</strong> Underground gutter drains handle roof runoff — water that hits your roof and flows through your gutter system. French drains handle groundwater — water already in the soil around your foundation. Different systems for different problems. Some Decatur properties need both. We assess what's actually going on with your water and recommend accordingly.</p>

<p>For Decatur installations, we design systems that work during flood conditions. If your property experiences standing water during TVA releases or heavy regional rainfall, discharge points are positioned to ensure water always has somewhere to go — even when surface conditions are wet.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed underground drainage throughout <a href="/recent-projects/">Morgan County</a> for over 20 years. We understand Decatur's unique challenges — the high water table, TVA-managed lake levels, flood patterns in different neighborhoods, and soil conditions that vary across the city.</p>

<p>Our systems are engineered for Decatur's reality. Quality materials, proper installation techniques, thoughtful design accounting for flood conditions. When you work with Blue River Gutters, you're getting local expertise backed by two decades in the Tennessee Valley.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> Every spring we dig up corrugated drainage pipe that failed. Homeowners installed it themselves or hired a landscaper who didn't know better. The pipe is clogged with sediment, roots have invaded the corrugations, and water isn't flowing. They end up paying twice — once for the failed system, once for us to replace it with smooth PVC. Do it right the first time.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> If you're spending money on underground drainage, spend the extra 20-30% for smooth PVC. The corrugated stuff isn't cheaper when you account for the fact that it fails and needs replacement within 5 years. This is one place where "saving money" doesn't save money — it just delays and doubles the expense.</p>
{% endset %}
